*
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
* SIMULATION (src/sim.f), as for Apollo 11.  Burn sizes and firing times
* from SP p. 46 (Apollo 8 translunar, lunar orbit and transearth phases);
* mid-burn g.e.t. is the ignition plus half the firing time (ours).  Left
* out, all below 2.5 ft/s or before the start: the separation manoeuvres
* (1.1 and 7.7 ft/s, MR8 p. 5-2) and the second midcourse
* correction (1.4 ft/s, SP p. 46).
*
START T=2:56:05.5 END=147:00:00 BODY=EARTH LATTYPE=GD LAT=21.48 LON=-143.02 ALT=187.1 V=35505 FPA=7.90 HDG=67.49 SRC="MR8 Table 5-II p. 5-7, translunar injection"
*
BURN T=11:00:00.4 DV=20.4 BODY=EARTH P=-0.798 R=0.576 N=0.174 SRC="SP p. 46: first midcourse correction ignition 010:59:59.2, 2.4 s, 20.4 ft/s. Direction derived by us from the MR8 Table 5-II p. 5-7 ignition and cutoff rows (their difference, less gravity over 2.4 s, is 18.4 ft/s)"
BURN T=69:10:23.85 DV=2997 BODY=MOON P=-1 R=0 N=0 SRC="SP p. 46: lunar orbit insertion ignition 069:08:20.4, 246.9 s, 2,997 ft/s. Direction against the velocity: our assumption"
BURN T=73:35:11.4 DV=134.8 BODY=MOON P=-1 R=0 N=0 SRC="SP p. 46: lunar orbit circularization ignition 073:35:06.6, 9.6 s, 134.8 ft/s. Direction against the velocity: our assumption"
BURN T=89:20:58.45 DV=3519.0 BODY=MOON P=1 R=0 N=0 SRC="SP p. 46: transearth injection ignition 089:19:16.6, 203.7 s, 3,519.0 ft/s. Direction along the velocity: our assumption"
BURN T=104:00:07.5 DV=4.8 BODY=EARTH P=-1 R=0 N=0 SRC="SP p. 46: midcourse correction ignition 104:00:00.00, 15.00 s, 4.8 ft/s; MR8 p. 5-3: to decrease the inertial velocity by 4.8 ft/sec"
*
* Reference rows: MR8 Table 5-II pp. 5-7, 5-8 and Table 5-V p. 5-10.
* Not used: the third midcourse correction ignition row (103:59:54).  Its
* flight-path angle, -80.59 deg 306,000 km out, puts the Earth-only conic's
* perigee 704 km below the surface (0.6 deg shallower gives the entry
* interface's 35 km); correcting to it left the engine 19,500 km from the
* entry interface row against 5,000 km without it.  The transearth coast is
* the engine's weak leg for Apollo 8: from the transearth injection cutoff,
* 57 h of free flight, it reaches entry thousands of km off (ours: a point
* mass Moon, an impulsive burn).
REF T=3:20:59 BODY=EARTH LATTYPE=GD LAT=25.86 LON=-66.23 ALT=3797.8 V=24975 FPA=45.11 HDG=107.12 SRC="spacecraft/S-IVB separation"
REF T=11:00:01.9 BODY=EARTH LATTYPE=GD LAT=-1.71 LON=-123.75 ALT=52771.7 V=8172 FPA=73.75 HDG=120.54 SRC="first midcourse correction cutoff"
REF T=69:12:27.3 BODY=MOON LAT=-9.89 LON=179.56 ALT=62.0 V=5458 FPA=-0.79 SRC="lunar orbit insertion cutoff"
REF T=73:35:16 BODY=MOON LAT=-11.66 LON=159.79 ALT=60.7 V=5345 FPA=-0.02 SRC="lunar orbit circularization cutoff"
REF T=89:22:40.3 BODY=MOON LAT=-11.17 LON=160.19 ALT=66.1 V=8842 FPA=5.10 SRC="transearth injection cutoff"
* REF T=103:59:54 BODY=EARTH LATTYPE=GD LAT=-5.67 LON=-57.27 ALT=165561.5 V=4299 FPA=-80.59 HDG=52.65 SRC="third midcourse correction ignition"
REF T=146:46:12.8 BODY=EARTH LATTYPE=GD LAT=20.83 LON=-179.89 ALT=65.90 V=36221 FPA=-6.50 HDG=121.57 SRC="MR8 Table 5-V p. 5-10, entry interface; flight-path angle printed unsigned"
*
* Burn cue (src/lburn.f; ours, a modern addition): the main-engine firings,
* each opened and closed by two TIMELINE rows of this scenario (SP-4029's
* timelines, by exact name; a pair whose names occur more than once takes the
* first ignition and the first cutoff after it), with whose engine it is.
* VEH=CSM|LM|S-IVB, ENG=SPS|DPS (descent)|APS (ascent)|J-2 (the S-IVB's).
* SRC says where the engine comes from.
* RCS firings and the S-IVB's APS slingshot burn are left out, as in
* ../apollo11/asflown.scn: the cue is for the main engines.
*
BURNCUE IGN="S-IVB 1st burn ignition" CUT="S-IVB 1st burn cutoff" VEH=S-IVB ENG=J-2 SRC="the rows name the stage"
BURNCUE IGN="S-IVB 2nd burn ignition" CUT="S-IVB 2nd burn cutoff" VEH=S-IVB ENG=J-2 SRC="the rows name the stage"
BURNCUE IGN="Midcourse correction ignition" CUT="Midcourse correction cutoff" VEH=CSM ENG=SPS SRC="ours: the BURN card's 20.4 ft/s in 2.4 s (SP p. 46) is beyond the SM RCS (the first such pair)"
BURNCUE IGN="Lunar orbit insertion ignition" CUT="Lunar orbit insertion cutoff" VEH=CSM ENG=SPS SRC="ours: the BURN card's 2,997 ft/s (SP p. 46) is the SPS's"
BURNCUE IGN="Lunar orbit circularization ignition" CUT="Lunar orbit circularization cutoff" VEH=CSM ENG=SPS SRC="ours: the BURN card's 134.8 ft/s in 9.6 s (SP p. 46) is beyond the SM RCS"
BURNCUE IGN="Transearth injection ignition (SPS)" CUT="Transearth injection cutoff" VEH=CSM ENG=SPS SRC="the row's SPS (SP p. 49)"
*
* SITUATIONS: card types as in ../apollo11/asflown.scn (its SITUATIONS
* section lists them).
*
* Apollo 8 Earthrise: at the photograph AS08-14-2383 (the PHOTO event), with
* the field of its 250 mm lens on the 70 mm frame, 12.72 deg: the lens from
* the Apollo 8 Flight Journal (day 4, orbit 4, commentary: "another
* Hasselblad with a 250-mm lens"), the 55.74 mm gate measured by us on the
* ASU scan of AS08-14-2383 (tothemoon.im-ldi.com), taking the 70 mm
* perforation pitch as 4.75 mm (unsourced).  Situation 1's forward horizon
* view, its azimuth set at the photograph's time, turned by three angles
* FITTED by us to the photograph as usually shown (the film frame a quarter
* turn clockwise): the Earth's centre at plot (0.8550, -0.4555) deg and the
* horizon falling 6.634 deg to the right, as measured on the scan
* (2026-09-30; CLAUDE.md, scene 9).  Away from lunar orbit (no LUNAR leg
* holds the g.e.t.: traj.f LUNIN) the camera looks forward 8 deg above the
* Earth's horizon and the default target is the Earth: our choice, since no
* Apollo 8 view survives (TN D-6853 printed p. 3).
SITUATION ID=1 NAME="APOLLO 8 EARTHRISE" TITLE="Apollo 8 Earthrise" GET=PHOTO FOV=12.72 WINDOW=CSM LAYERS=FRAME,STARS,SUN,MOON,EARTH,VEHICLES,BURN SRC="SVS 4129 (AS08-14-2383 at 75:48:39.7); Apollo 8 Flight Journal day 4 orbit 4 (250 mm lens); the gate measured by us"
RECIPE NAME=LOCALVERT BODY=MOON MODE=FORWARD AZ=EARTH ELEV=0 TURN=-0.9043,3.7599,-6.7873 OFFLEG=EARTH OFFELEV=8 SRC="TURN fitted by us to the ASU scan of AS08-14-2383; OFFLEG ours"
VIEWS VIEW=WINDOW TARGET=MOON OFFTARGET=EARTH RIDES=CSM CM=ALWAYS LM=PLACED
*
* SPANS: card types as in ../apollo11/asflown.scn (its SPANS section lists
* them).  No LIVE, JUMP or PIN cards: Live follows Apollo 11.
*
* Follow: situation 1 rides the LUNAR legs (LUNORB) with the Earthrise
* camera, and the Earth legs with Apollo 11's situation 3's view above the
* horizon (its RECIPE card's OFFLEG=, vdrive.f SCNCAM, traj.f LUNIN).  The
* 50 deg field of the window views is MSC IN 69-FM-197's for the CSM window
* aimed at the Earth on Apollo 11's transearth coast (figure 7.3.2-1,
* "constant field of view (earth)", printed p. 207).
*
* Captions (#35; ours): situation 1 is the Earthrise camera only in lunar orbit,
* so each span outside it names its phase (CAPTION=), else the page would
* caption the parking orbit or the entry "Apollo 8 Earthrise".  The phase
* names are MR8's section 5 headings (its contents): 5.1 Launch phase
* (p. 5-1), 5.2 Earth parking orbit (p. 5-1), 5.3 Translunar injection
* (p. 5-2), 5.5 Lunar orbit insertion (p. 5-3), 5.6 Transearth injection
* (p. 5-3), 5.8 Entry (p. 5-3); and its summary's "translunar and
* transearth coast phases" (section 1.0).  Which span gets
* which name is ours.  The lunar orbit span keeps the situation's own
* title, Apollo 8 Earthrise.
*
* The pad and the ascent (TABLE leg, SP p. 45) to S-IVB cutoff, where the
* CIRC leg begins (FROM 0:11:24.98).
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=1 UNTIL=0:11:24.98 VIEW=EXTERNAL TARGET=EARTH FOV=20 CAPTION="Apollo 8 launch phase"
* The CIRC leg to the S-IVB's 2nd burn ignition, 2:50:37.79 (SP p. 48): the
* horizon view at situation 3's 70 deg.
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=1 UNTIL=2:50:37.79 FOV=70 CAPTION="Apollo 8 Earth parking orbit"
* TLI, to its cutoff 2:55:55.51 (SP p. 48): the CM station at 100 deg, the
* Apollo 11 note's TLI format (figure 5.1-1).
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=1 UNTIL=2:55:55.51 VIEW=CM FOV=100 CAPTION="Apollo 8 translunar injection"
* The CIRC leg stretched to TLI (the TLI event, MR8 Table 5-II p. 5-7).
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=1 UNTIL=TLI FOV=70 CAPTION="Apollo 8 translunar injection"
* The TLI conic while the Earth's disc overfills the window's 50 deg field:
* 50 deg across at about 8150 nmi, 3:27 (our run).
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=1 UNTIL=3:27:00 VIEW=EXTERNAL TARGET=EARTH FOV=20 CAPTION="Apollo 8 translunar coast"
* The translunar conics to LOI ignition (the first LUNAR leg's FROM; the
* LOI1 event, MR8 Table 3-I p. 3-3).
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=1 UNTIL=LOI1 TARGET=EARTH FOV=50 CAPTION="Apollo 8 translunar coast"
* LOI, to its cutoff 69:12:27.3 (SP p. 49): the CM station at 100 deg, the
* Apollo 11 note's LOI format (figure 6.1-1), the Moon's limb across the
* frame (no LM).
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=1 UNTIL=69:12:27.3 VIEW=CM FOV=100 CAPTION="Apollo 8 lunar orbit insertion"
* Lunar orbit to TEI ignition (the last LUNAR leg's TO; the TEI event, MR8
* Table 3-I p. 3-3).
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=1 UNTIL=TEI
* TEI, to its cutoff 89:22:40.3 (SP p. 49): the CM station at 100 deg, the
* Apollo 11 note's TEI format (figure 7.1-1).
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=1 UNTIL=89:22:40.3 VIEW=CM FOV=100 CAPTION="Apollo 8 transearth injection"
* The transearth conics to entry interface (the EI event, MR8 Table 3-I p.
* 3-4).
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=1 UNTIL=EI TARGET=EARTH FOV=50 CAPTION="Apollo 8 transearth coast"
* The entry (TABLE leg, MR8 Tables 5-V and 6.9-IV) to splashdown, 147:00:42,
* then the splash point.
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=1 UNTIL=END VIEW=EXTERNAL TARGET=EARTH FOV=20 CAPTION="Apollo 8 entry"
